sec.p:
To elucidate the effects of hydrophobicity and the flexible substrate‐binding loop on 3α‐HSD/CR catalysis, we further compare the activation energies of WT and L193G mutant 3α‐HSD/CRs for androsterone, 2‐decalol, and cyclohexanol, respectively (Figure 5). As the hydrophobicity (πR) of residue G193 to L193 increases from 0 to 1.7, the difference in the activation energy (ΔΔG

‡
) decreases by 1.9, 3.2, and 4.5 kcal/mol for the substrates of cyclohexanol, 2‐decalol, and androsterone, respectively. Moreover, compared to cyclohexanol, the remote binding energy from the B‐ and BCD‐rings of androsterone lowers the activation energy by 0.8 and 5.9 kcal/mol for the L193G mutant, while 2.1 and 8.5 kcal/mol for WT 3α‐HSD/CR, respectively. Both the remote binding and the proximity hydrophobic interactions of androsterone with L193 residue synergistically contribute to the 3α‐HSD/CR catalytic efficiency, supported by the simulation of the ternary complexes of 3α‐HSD/CR, which shows a closed form with the differential interaction of L193 and the substrate‐binding loop with androsterone, 2‐decalol, and cyclohexanol. Together, these findings show that local hydrophobic contacts from L193 and remote interactions from the steroid scaffold act cooperatively to lower the activation energy and enhance catalysis.

caption.p:
Both the proximity hydrophobic interactions and the remote binding interactions from L193 and androsterone synergistically contribute to the 3α‐HSD/CR catalytic efficiency. Increases in the hydrophobicity at residue 193 from G to L, the change in the activation energy (ΔΔG

‡
) is −1.9, −3.2, and − 4.5 kcal/mol for WT 3α‐HSD/CR acting on cyclohexanol, 2‐decalol, and androsterone, respectively, compared to the L193G mutant. Increasing the remote binding interaction through the B‐ring and BCD‐ring of androsterone decreases the activation energy by 0.8 and 5.9 kcal/mol for the L193G mutant and 2.1 and 8.5 kcal/mol for the WT 3α‐HSD/CR, respectively. The activation energy (ΔG

‡
) for WT and L193G mutant 3α‐HSD/CR reacting with cyclohexanol, 2‐decalol, and androsterone is from Table 2 with the unit of kcal/mol.
